        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>It has long been known that microgravity has a degenerative effect upon bone cell formation. Previous research suggests that bone loss is due to a decrease in bone cell formation. Therefore, this study focuses on the response of osteoblast to both microgravity and a return to Earth. Gene expression, matrix production, and cell function were monitored during the 17 day mission and after landing.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>hFOB 1.19 were used on the 17 day STS-80 mission, which is a conditionally immortalized line that expresses the mature osteoblastic phenotype. Cells were housed in the Cell Culture Module (CCM). Standard conditions were used to culture the hFOB cell line, except that they were incubated at 37 degrees C, instead of at 34 degrees C. For each experimental cartridge, cells were attached by gently mixing 600,000 cells with 50 mg of Cytodex beads and incubating them for 45 minutes in 15 ml conical tubes with 5 ml of equilibrated DME/F12+10%FBS+G418 at 37 degrees C. The cartridges were then inserted into the CCM and incubated for 2 days prior to launch. 4 ml fractions were collected on days 1, 4, 8, and 12 from both flight cultures and ground control cultures. After recovery, the ends of the cartridges were cut and hollow fiber bundles were removed. The cells, beads, and collagenous matrix was scraped into 1X PBS, and RNA was isolated using the method of Chomczynski. After ethanol precipitation, RNA pellets were quantitated using UV spectrophotometry for Northern or RNase protection assays. For the Northern assays, total RNA isolated was denatured, separated by agarose gel electrophoresis, and bound to nylon filter by vacuum baking. After being hybridized, blots were exposed to phosphoimager screens. With a Molecular Dynamics Phosphoimager, band intensities were quantitated. In the RNase protection assay, RNA was hybridized, treated, separated by gel electrophoresis, and vacuum dried. Band intensities were quantitated on a phosphoimager screen. Procollagen was quantitated in a gamma counter and was then compared to a standard curve of known PICP standards. Enzyme immunoassay was used to measure the level of prostaglandin E2 in the hFOB conditioned media. A small sample of cells and beads was fixed for electron microscopy.<P><B>Results:</B><P>Results suggest that hFOB cells responded to space flight primarily in cytokine gene expression rather than in cell function and proliferation. It is especially notable that hFOB cells showed vigorous growth during flight. Gene expression, glucose utilization, and PGE2 and type I collagen accumulation were not significantly affected by prolonged exposure to microgravity. In microgravity, both type I collagen accumulation and PGE2 accumulations were unaffected. Flight and ground control cells showed no qualitative difference in morphology. These results suggest that hFOB cells may have a reduced response to microgravity in relation to rat bone cells. Further analysis suggests that dynamic loading is the dominant factor in regulating bone mass over static loading.
        </div>

